ether  and say to the team and this is what  amari cooper and dak prescott could say  we're not signing a contract until after  next year's deadline for the franchise  tag you will tag one of us and the other  one will become a free agent and between  cooper and prescott chances are it's  prescott who would be tagged and cooper  would then become a free agent but you  know he's going to carry the injury risk  for one more year but there's an upside  waiting for amari cooper and that's  ultimately why he has no problem waiting  for that long term deal  tom brady's still waiting for his next  long-term deal or as the case may be a  short-term deal he's in the final season  of the latest team-friendly contract  that he did with the patriots and it  always makes me laugh to call it team  friendly because he usually gets a  gigantic pile of money up front last  time around it was a 30 million signing  bonus but still  annual average far below what the best  quarterbacks in the nfl are making and  it was reported by nfl media on monday  that there's no extension in sight for  brady now he's got the same  quirk that applies to julio jones they  have to wait one year  after last year's  adjustment that was done by tom brady  remember last year he was given the  opportunity to earn up to five million  dollars in incentives based on five  different categories and it was a  million per category and he ultimately  hit  none  of those triggers so he got no extra  money last year even though the patriots  were trying to give it to him and look  after this year he becomes a free agent  now the patriots presumably would apply  the franchise tag if they want to keep  him around but  you you just have to wonder what kind of  late career contractual acrimony could  ultimately happen between the patriots  and brady especially because there's a  persistent belief and tom curran of nbc  sports boston is the one who clued me in  on this that brady wants to get paid as  much or more  than 